--cpp17
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ instance re-elaborated when completed: SUCCESS$
^VERIFICATION SUCCESSFUL$
^EXIT=0$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
--
An instance created at a declaration-only point (incomplete argument
type) is cached and served stale after the type is completed and the
specialization is used ([temp.point]: the use is a new point of
instantiation).  The mem-initializer variant SEGFAULTS in
symex_assign.  Split from cpp17_incomplete_template_arg_decl.
Investigated 2026-07-28: a staleness-remark mechanism
(#had_incomplete_arg on the instance, re-elaboration at the later
point of instantiation) re-runs the instantiation but the re-run
never rebuilds the BASE list (bases()=0 across all elaborations): the
template's stored declaration loses the base-clause information after
the first instantiation, so re-elaboration needs the body/base-clause
preserved first.  Deeper than the cache policy.
Round-6 findings (2026-08-02): three re-elaboration strategies fail
the same way -- at any later point of instantiation the base-specifier
`T` no longer RESOLVES (resolve of the template parameter throws even
with the template map built and parameter ids copied into the class
scope), so the rebuild drops the base again or produces an empty
class.  The fix needs the base-specifier resolution context of the
ORIGINAL instantiation to be reconstructible; parked.
